# Every five minutes.
#
# It was every minute, and the argument for that still mostly holds: a pass with
# nothing armed makes NO model call — it is a filesystem scan and a `git pull` —
# and what the cadence buys is latency, which is the whole feel of the loop. Arm
# a capture on a phone and the proposal is waiting by the time you put it down.
#
# What changed is the second half of the old comment, which said an interval is
# the wrong place to bound cost: "lengthening it makes a runaway slower without
# making it smaller, and makes the good case worse." The first clause is true
# and the conclusion did not follow. A runaway ran at one billed call per minute
# for 56 hours — 3189 of them — because a write failure meant no guard could
# persist. Slower would have meant five times less of it before anyone looked.
#
# The real ceiling now lives in `usage.ts` as a daily token cap, outside the
# vault, which is the fix for "without making it smaller". This interval is the
# other half: it bounds the RATE at which any future bug can spend before that
# cap notices, and it makes the good case worse by about four minutes.
#
# AccuracySec stays explicit because systemd's default is a whole minute.
[Unit]
Description=Run a braindance applier pass every five minutes

[Timer]
OnBootSec=2min
OnUnitActiveSec=5min
AccuracySec=15s
Persistent=true

[Install]
WantedBy=timers.target
